{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-review-c4b.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 81 reviewed the constructed-history scenario, which removes the last assumption from the crash finding.\\n\\nState on entry. The artifact 31f09787edee4acb89ef8562412eccb2 (executed as ae38ca8c…) reported violated with the oracle correspondence unreviewed; its stream shows the whole history: proposal_before_restart with bookkeeping present, one pending proposal and status PREACCEPTED; after_restart with no record; record_from_message with bookkeeping nil; and handle_commit_result with panicked true, the nil-pointer dereference and no commit effect.\\n\\nReview judgement (checker_correspondence, no_issue_found). Requirement versus predicate: the claim is that a legal single-NONE commit for an instance the replica owns must be processed without dereferencing absent bookkeeping and without terminating the replica, and the predicate asserts panicked == false; the run reports true, evaluated on printed fields. History and legality: the owner admits a client operation with the real handlePropose, so the instance exists with bookkeeping and a pending proposal; the restart is then modelled by constructing a fresh replica with no durable state, which is exactly what the shipped configuration's durable=false implies; the recovery-led PreAccept recreates the record without bookkeeping, and the commit follows. Identity: the prerequisite record event and the commit result share instance 0.0. Oracle and limits: the panic is observed by wrapping only the commit delivery in recover, and the process-termination step remains an inference from the run loop having no recover rather than an observation, as does the modelled restart (a construction of the state the configuration permits rather than an executed crash-restart cycle). No counterevidence was found that the handler survives in this scope.\\n\\nFrontier. The crash family now reads as